2026-06-30 15:30:48,307 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-0db8d561-064d-41d4-9ddb-c04493187609', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: sex\nsdrf_column: characteristics[sex]\nontology: PATO:0000047\ndescription: Biological sex of the donor.\nallowed_values: male, female, intersex, hermaphrodite, anonymized, not available, not applicable\ninstructions: Use one of the fixed values above. Return "not available" if not stated.\n  Return "not applicable" if the sample is from a cell line or organism without defined sex.\n\nterm: age\nsdrf_column: characteristics[age]\nontology: EFO:0000246\ndescription: Age of the donor at sample collection.\npattern: {Number}{Unit} where Unit is Y (years), M (months), W (weeks), D (days).\n  Also allowed: "anonymized" if age is stated but anonymized.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: ancestry category\nsdrf_column: characteristics[ancestry category]\nontology: HANCESTRO\ndescription: Ancestry or ethnic background of the donor.\ninstructions: Use HANCESTRO terms if possible. Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: biological replicate\nsdrf_column: characteristics[biological replicate]\nontology: MS:1001809\ndescription: Identifier for the biological replicate (integer or "pooled").\ninstructions: Return a description of the replication strategy from the paper\n  (e.g. "3 biological replicates", "2 independent experiments"). The actual\n  integer values are assigned per-file automatically; describe what you find in the text.\n\nterm: genotype\nsdrf_column: characteristics[genotype]\nontology: EFO:0000513\ndescription: Known genetic variant, mutation, or genotype of the subject.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Example: "wild type", "Δatg5", "ΔYme1".\n\nterm: pooled sample\nsdrf_column: characteristics[pooled sample]\nontology: NCIT:C165587\ndescription: Whether the sample is a pooled sample.\nallowed_values: not pooled, pooled, or pattern SN=sample1;SN=sample2\ninstructions: Return "not pooled" unless explicitly stated otherwise.\n\nterm: phosphate\nsdrf_column: characteristics[phosphate]\nontology: PRIDE:0000805\ndescription: Phosphate concentration (only relevant for environmental/water samples).\npattern: number + mg/L or umol/L\ninstructions: Return null unless this is a water/environmental sample with explicit\n  phosphate concentration stated.\n\nterm: temperature\nsdrf_column: characteristics[temperature]\nontology: PRIDE:0000703\ndescription: Temperature at the sampling location (environmental samples only).\npattern: number + °C\ninstructions: Return null unless this is an environmental/metaproteomics sample\n  with explicit temperature stated. Do NOT confuse with crosslinking temperature.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nA Spatiotemporal Notch Interaction Map from Membrane to Nucleus\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nNotch signaling relies on ligand-induced proteolysis to liberate a nuclear effector that drives cell fate decisions. The location and timing of individual steps required for proteolysis and movement of Notch from membrane to nucleus, however, remain unclear. Here, we use proximity labeling with quantitative multiplexed mass spectrometry to monitor the microenvironment of endogenous Notch2 after ligand stimulation in the presence of a gamma secretase inhibitor and then as a function of time after inhibitor removal. Our studies show that gamma secretase cleavage of Notch2 occurs in an intracellular compartment and that formation of nuclear complexes and recruitment of chromatin-modifying enzymes occurs within 45 minutes of inhibitor washout. This work provides a spatiotemporal map of unprecedented detail tracking the itinerary of Notch from membrane to nucleus after activation and identifies molecular events in signal transmission that are potential targets for modulating Notch signaling activity.\n\n[METHODS]\nOn-bead digestion and tandem mass tag (TMT) labeling The streptavidin beads were subjected to on-bead protease digestion in 50 μl digestion buffer (200mM EPPS pH 8.5 with 2% acetonitrile [v/v]) along with LysC (Wako) at an enzyme-to-substrate ratio of 1:50. The samples were incubated at 37°C for 3 h. Then 50 μl of digestion buffer with trypsin (Promega) was added at an enzyme-to-substrate ratio of 1:100. The digestion was continued at 37˚C overnight with gentle agitation. The clear supernatants of digested protein were separated from beads with a magnetic rack and transferred to fresh tubes. For the TMT reaction, 30% acetonitrile (v/v) was added to the digested protein and then labeled using a TMT isobaric mass tagging kit (ThermoFisher Scientific). The TMT reaction was performed for 1 h according to the manufacturer’s instructions. TMT labeling efficiency and ratios were measured by LC-MS3 analysis after combining equal volumes from each sample. Once the labeling efficiency was determined to be >95%, the TMT reactions were quenched with hydroxylamine 0.5% v/v for 15 min and acidified with formic acid. Samples were then pooled and dried to near completion under reduced pressure before resuspension in 1% formic acid and fractionation using a Pierce High pH Reversed Phase Peptide Fractionation Kit (ThermoFisher Scientific) with modified elution of 12 sequential fractions (10%, 12.5%, 15%, 17.5%, 20%, 25%, 30%, 35%, 40%, 50%, 65% and 80% acetonitrile). Fractions were then combined into pairs as follows, 1+7, 2+8, 3+9, 4+10, 5+11, 6+12, to give the final six fractionated samples. The resulting fractions were dried under reduced pressure and then desalted using a stage tip protocol.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
